    <text type="letter">
        <body>
            <div type="writingSession">
                <pb n="1"/>
                <opener><dateline><date when="1946-02-26">February 26,
                        1946</date></dateline><salute>Dear <rs ref="#tillich_person_id__1928"
                            type="person">Paul Tillich</rs>,</salute>
                </opener>
                <p>Please forgive this bad typing job: I just got out of the hospital and my hands
                    don&apos;t follow the instructions of my brain.</p>
                <p>There seems to be a chance that THE NATION may need a motion picture reviewer.
                    Now, you know my background pretty thoroughly but I&apos;m inclined to think it a
                    good idea to give you some details so you will be able to talk to <rs
                        ref="#tillich_person_id__1390" type="person">Dr. Niebuhr</rs>.</p>
                <p>I have written two books on the movies: one published in <rs
                        ref="#tillich_place_id__480" type="place">Zürich</rs>, <rs
                        ref="#tillich_place_id__397" type="place">Switzerland</rs>, in 1938 under
                    the title <rs ref="#tillich__SHXHG2FB" type="work">POCKET GUIDE TO THE
                        MOVIES</rs>, and one for Row, Peterson &amp; Co, <rs
                        ref="#tillich_place_id__830" type="place"><sic>Evanstown</sic></rs>, <rs
                        ref="#tillich_place_id__999" type="place">Ill.</rs>, which will be published
                    soon. Title: <rs ref="#tillich__" type="work">THE
                    MOVIES</rs>.<!-- Nicht gefunden. --> This is a book on motion pictures
                    appreciation for high schools.</p>
                <p>For two years I lectured at the <sic>university</sic> of <rs
                        ref="#tillich_place_id__31" type="place">Basel</rs> on the technique, the
                    history and appreciation of films. I wrote articles for THEATRE ARTS, THE NATION
                    and other magazines, all dealing with movies, and for one year I was regular
                    motion picture editor of DECISION.</p>
                <p>I also gave several lectures at the Film Library of the Museum of Modern Art.
                        (<rs ref="#tillich_person_id__3455" type="person">Iris Barry</rs>,
                    director).</p>
                <p>As far as my other writing and editing activities are concerned, I think the
                    enclosed curriculum will tell you the most important facts. Most of it you know
                    anyhow. I gave up the job of book editor of TOMORROW because it was a fulltime
                    job, and what with my novel, I could manage only a half time position.</p>
                <p>Perhaps I could talk with <rs ref="#tillich_person_id__3457" type="person">Mrs.
                        Kirchwey</rs> one of these days, if it can be arranged without too much
                    trouble. I&apos;d be extremely grateful to you. Let me add that <rs
                        ref="#tillich_person_id__3456" type="person">Bosley Crowther</rs>, motion
                    picture editor of THE TIMES, and <rs ref="#tillich_person_id__3455"
                        type="person">Iris Barry</rs> of the Film Library can be used as references.
                    And thanks a lot. I hope your hand will be allright soon.</p>
                <closer><salute>Best wishes yours,</salute><signed><rs
                            ref="#tillich_person_id__1511" type="person">Richard</rs></signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
